    "body": "### Problem\n\nWhen a window is closed and the frame that receives its space is a group of windows rather than a single window, the space goes to the outermost window of that group. This happens even if that window has 'winfixwidth' set and another window in the same row has not. 'winfixheight' behaves the same way in the transposed layout.\n\n`:help 'winfixwidth'` says the width is kept when windows are opened or closed with 'equalalways' set, and only changes when running out of room. Here there is room: C has no 'winfixwidth' and could take the space. If A's direct neighbour is B itself (no D in the steps below), C does get the space.\n\nReal-world case: nvim-dap-ui opens a bottom panel, then a full-height sidebar on the left. A file tree with 'winfixwidth' (neo-tree) sits next to the editor in the remaining block. Closing the sidebar while the bottom panel is still open roughly doubles the file tree's width.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\nSave as `repro.vim` and run `nvim --clean -S repro.vim`, then `:messages`:\n\n```vim\n\" B has 'winfixwidth', C is a normal window, D spans the bottom, A spans the left.\nvsplit | vertical resize 30 | setlocal winfixwidth | let w:name = 'B'\nwincmd l | let w:name = 'C'\nbotright split | let w:name = 'D'\ntopleft vsplit | vertical resize 20 | let w:name = 'A'\n\nfunction! Widths() abort\n  return join(map(range(1, winnr('$')), {_, n -> getwinvar(n, 'name') .. '=' .. winwidth(n)}), ' ')\nendfunction\n\nechomsg 'before:' Widths()\nclose\nechomsg 'after: ' Widths()\n```\n\nOutput with 120 columns:\n\n```\nbefore: A=20 B=30 C=68 D=99\nafter:  B=51 C=68 D=120\n```\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nB keeps its width and C takes the freed columns:\n\n```\nafter:  B=30 C=89 D=120\n```\n\nPossible cause: `winframe_find_altwin()` correctly picks the group `col[row[B, C], D]` to receive the space. `winframe_remove()` then calls `frame_new_width(altfr, ..., leftfirst, false)`. Because `wfw` is false, the extra columns go to the first child of `row[B, C]` without skipping B. Passing `true` would skip B, but `frame_new_width()` gives up when every frame in a row is fixed, so that case would need a fallback. `frame_new_height()` and 'winfixheight' have the same pattern.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.12.5. The relevant code in `winframe_remove()` is unchanged on master.\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nNot tested. Vim master has the same call in `winframe_remove()` (`frame_new_width(frp2, ..., FALSE)`), so probably yes. Related: vim/vim#2998, fixed by patch 8.1.1088 for a similar case.\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nArch Linux (Omarchy), kernel 7.2.5\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nNot terminal-specific, also reproduces with `--headless`.\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-256color\n\n### Installation\n\nArch package (neovim 0.12.5-1)",
